archmage84 wrote:
>
> So let me get this straight: Expert for normal melee weapons is stupid
> unless using #twoweapon (to minimise the penalty), Expert for daggers
> is a very good thing, Expert spellcasting schools are excellent too
> (with exceptions as noted by the Wizard page on Wikihack), and
> #twoweapon is generally awesome?

It's extreme but accurate enough.

Because the number of missiles launched increases with skilled and
expert there is a benefit to each and every enhancement gives
significant benefit. Being able to fire 3 daggers or 4 arrows is a
gigantic difference. The improvement for both melee and spellcasting
is much smaller.

For missile weapons if your class can get multi-fire it is worth it to
push those classes to max. Every class that can get to Expert in dagger
should very early. Every class that can get to Expert in bow should
consider it early and should go for it as soon as they are able to max
out luck and bless stacks of arrows.

Missile combat is powerful enough that it's worth it to me to enhance
dagger to basic in some classes even if they can never multi-fire in
dagger.  Being able to kill several of an advancing swarm is
great.  Being able to do so much damage a strong monster dies in
a hit or two once it gets to melee range is very great.

For both melee and spell casting the move from Skilled to Expert does
not change the behavior or bonuses enough compared to the high cost in
skill slots.  Early game folks are tempted to enhance and it's a trap in
the early game.  Several spells change behavior at Skilled but none
change behavior at Expert. For spell casting it's a matter of failure
percentages and that's handled by other means.  In the late game if you
have the slots it's no longer a trap.

> And they need to balance skill bonuses?

Skill slots are a limited resource.  They need to be planned ahead using
strategy.  Treating them tactically nor strategically is very expensive.


